# [HOTRFC](../wg/hotrfc.html)

## Summary

The HOTRFC (How-Others-Talk RFC) session at IETF 127 provided a rapid-fire forum for presenters to introduce early-stage research, experimental ideas, and proposals for community feedback and collaboration. Session chair Heather Flanagan opened the meeting, reminding participants of the IETF Note Well guidelines and pointing attendees to the IAB New Work Help Desk. Each presentation was strictly limited to four minutes, with no live Q&A during the session. Topics covered a broad spectrum including CoAP congestion control, AI agent identity and trust, data center transport protocols, post-quantum DNSSEC extensions, network measurement, BGP authorization overhead, local LLM service discovery, HTTP proxy rate limiting, digital sovereignty in confidential computing, and outreach to early-career engineers.

## Key Discussion Points

### Session Overview and Rules
- **Chair Opening**: Heather Flanagan introduced the rules for the session: 4 minutes per talk, strict timing, no live Q&A, and encouraged follow-up conversations during hallway tracks, side meetings, or via email. Attendees were reminded of the IETF Note Well and directed to the IAB New Work Help Desk located at registration.

### Technical Presentations

* **[01 CoCoA++: A Delay Gradient-Based Extension to CoAP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-01-cocoa-a-delay-gradient-based-extension-to-coap-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Mohit
  * **Discussion**: Mohit presented an enhancement to the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP, RFC 7252) retransmission timeout (RTO) calculation. Building on previous work in the COCOA draft, CoCoA++ utilizes delay gradients (differences between minimum and maximum RTT samples) rather than single RTT samples to determine a probability of backoff (PBF). The RTO is scaled by 1.42 when the calculated backoff probability exceeds a randomized threshold. Experimental evaluations on the FIT IoT-Lab testbed across static and mobile topologies demonstrated reductions in maximum and average RTO and improved successful transmission rates. The authors are seeking collaborators for real-world deployment testing.

* **[02 Agent-to-Agent Trust, Identity, and Verifiable Provenance](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-02-agent-to-agent-trust-identity-and-verifiable-provenance-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Tony Trujillo
  * **Discussion**: Tony Trujillo introduced `draft-trujillo-ai-agent-trust-04`, addressing verifiable provenance for multi-hop AI agent chains spanning organizational boundaries. Each agent is instantiated from a signed template, with delegation tokens bound to presenting agent certificates and evaluated at each hop. Token scope can only narrow, never widen. The author noted three running implementations and requested review from the WIMSE (Workload Identity in Multi-System Environments) and Web Bot Auth communities.

* **[Homa](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-homa-00)**
  * **Presenter**: John Ousterhout
  * **Discussion**: John Ousterhout presented Homa, a clean-slate, connectionless transport protocol designed specifically for data center environments. Homa replaces TCP’s byte-stream model with a reliable RPC request-response message framework. By avoiding TCP's in-order stream constraints and traditional congestion control bottlenecks, Homa delivers 10x to 100x lower tail latency for short messages under mixed workloads and high network utilization (relevant for storage systems and AI coordination workloads). An Internet-Draft is in progress alongside an open-source GitHub implementation.

* **[How Merkle Tree Ladders Help Address PQC DNSSEC Challenges](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-how-merkle-tree-ladders-help-address-pqc-dnssec-challenges-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Houyin Song
  * **Discussion**: Houyin Song presented Merkle Tree Ladders (MTL) to address payload expansion in DNSSEC when transitioning to Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) algorithms such as MLDSA. MTL aggregates zone record sets into a ladder authenticated by a single large PQC signature, allowing individual record sets to be verified using small Merkle proofs. An optional EDNS0 extension (`SigTag`) enables resolvers to signal already known signatures, eliminating redundant signature transmissions and permitting responses to fit within UDP payload limits. Code and interactive Jupyter notebooks are available on GitHub.

* **[Verdict records: checkable results on AI agent outputs](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-verdict-records-checkable-results-on-ai-agent-outputs-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Darren
  * **Discussion**: Darren detailed `draft-verdict-records-02`, a specification defining verifiable records of evaluation judgments rendered on AI agent outputs. Verdict records bind evaluation conclusions to underlying evidence (such as receipt hashes) and derivation rules, allowing online third-party verification without querying the original issuer. Two independent external implementations matched test vector outputs. Input was solicited on suitable WG placement (SCITT, SPICE, or DISPATCH).

* **[Network Support for Reverse Traceroute](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-network-support-for-reverse-traceroute-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Italo Cunha
  * **Discussion**: Italo Cunha addressed the operational challenge of diagnosing network latency inflation caused by asymmetric reverse-path detours when control over the destination endpoint is unavailable. Current mechanisms are limited: IPv4 Record Route options are frequently dropped by routers per BCP guidance and do not exist in IPv6. The author advocated for standardized IETF support for reverse traceroute designed to prevent DoS amplification/reflection while avoiding operational overhead on core routers.

* **[Do Networks Need Prefix-Specific Provider Authorization?](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-do-networks-need-prefix-specific-provider-authorization-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Stephanie
  * **Discussion**: Stephanie presented empirical analysis evaluating whether Autonomous System Provider Authorization (ASPA) requires prefix-specific authorization lists rather than global per-AS provider lists. BGP data revealed ~60,000 origin ASes exhibiting selective announcements or scrubbing behavior, with ~20,000 having profiles suitable for prefix-specific rules. Benchmarking showed ASPA-prefix increases route processing time by 25%, while RPA with origin specification increases processing time by 35%. Feedback from network operators was solicited regarding real-world requirements.

* **[Discovery of LLM API Services in Local Networks](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-discovery-of-llm-api-services-in-local-networks-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Yihan Chow
  * **Discussion**: Yihan Chow presented an Internet-Draft defining automated local network discovery for LLM API services. Designed for roaming devices moving between enterprise and home environments, the scheme leverages mDNS and DNS-SD (`_lmapi._tcp.local`) using PTR, SRV, TXT, and A/AAAA records to discover candidate base URLs, interface profiles, and transport bindings. Discovered endpoints require explicit user or local policy approval prior to connection.

* **[audits.json: Automated Discovery of Audit Reports](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-auditsjson-automated-discovery-of-audit-reports-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Daniel Kahn Gillmor
  * **Discussion**: Daniel Kahn Gillmor outlined a proposal to establish a standardized `.well-known/audits.json` location and JSON schema for discovering mandatory public audit reports (e.g., algorithmic bias audits required by New York City law, California Privacy Act, and EU regulations). The specification strictly handles discovery and location indexing, remaining agnostic regarding report contents or audit validity. The proposal was also presented at the DISPATCH WG.

* **[SCITT-Based Cryptographic Continuity for Digital Assets](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-scitt-based-cryptographic-continuity-for-digital-assets-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Brandon Davis
  * **Discussion**: Brandon Davis attempted to present remotely on applying Supply Chain Integrity, Transparency, and Trust (SCITT) architectures to distinguish authorized digital asset recovery decisions from basic signature validation. Due to severe audio quality degradation, the presentation was curtailed by the chairs; slides and abstract links were shared via the session agenda and chat.

* **[One suite, N verifiers](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-one-suite-n-verifiers-00)** *(Presented under topic: Identity is self assertive behavior isn't)*
  * **Presenter**: Abdel Fain
  * **Discussion**: Abdel Fain presented findings from honeypot deployments and Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers, revealing that incoming AI agents regularly executed sensitive tool calls (shell commands, database queries, reading secrets) without declaring agent identities. He emphasized that static identity is insufficient for prompt-injection threats and advocated for zero-trust runtime authorization, short-lived scoped grants, local credential signing, and standardized metrics for agent runtime misbehavior.

* **[CroakOS: Engineering Impacts of a Distributed Operating System](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-croakos-engineering-impacts-of-a-distributed-operating-system-00)**
  * **Presenter**: John Fawcett
  * **Discussion**: John Fawcett presented CroakOS, an open-source distributed operating system providing single-writer shared RAM and deterministic event scheduling over WireGuard overlays across the Internet. By replacing messaging plumbing with a stateful lockless memory architecture, conversions of existing systems showed substantial code reductions and performance gains, including an 80% code reduction and up to 330x speedup in distributed PyTorch mesh training, as well as a 70% code reduction in lispers.net.

* **[Client Groups for Shared HTTP connection](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-client-groups-for-shared-http-connection-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Santosh
  * **Discussion**: Santosh presented a proposal enabling HTTP proxies and intermediaries to tag requests with client group identifiers. When clients share multiplexed connections (HTTP/2 or HTTP/3) behind a proxy, origins currently cannot distinguish individual clients, leading to coarse IP blocking or connection drops during abusive events. The proposal extends work in the HTTPAPI working group (`draft-ietf-httpapi-ratelimit-headers`) by passing partition keys so origins can enforce targeted rate limits on specific client groups.

* **[Rules as Code for AI Agents](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-rules-as-code-for-ai-agents-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Tom Sato
  * **Discussion**: Tom Sato discussed enterprise challenges in ensuring AI agents comply with internal corporate policies and external regulatory requirements. Highlighting fragmented efforts across initiatives such as Rules as Code, OpenFisca, Catala, and OECD, he called for discussion on whether software and network standardization bodies should define formal policy frameworks for agent enforcement.

* **[Originator Profile Framework Update](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-originator-profile-framework-update-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Shigeya Suzuki
  * **Discussion**: Shigeya Suzuki updated the community on the Originator Profile (OP) framework, which delivers validated third-party attributes for web content and email (via S/MIME attachments). Recent technical additions include key rollover procedures, profile annotation revocation mechanisms, and DNS-based key status signaling (discussed in DNSOP). Active deployments include news article verification and local government emergency response communications in Japan.

* **[Defensive Trust in Generative AI Research](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-defensive-trust-in-generative-ai-research-00)**
  * **Presenter**: James Olorundare
  * **Discussion**: James Olorundare proposed a zero-trust verification model and evaluation methodology for generative AI outputs in academic and technical research. The framework aims to detect hallucinations and provide reproducible audit trails to ensure research integrity when AI tools are integrated into scientific workflows.

* **[Confidential Computing and Digital Sovereignty](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-confidential-computing-and-digital-sovereignty-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Muhammad Usama Sardar
  * **Discussion**: Muhammad Usama Sardar analyzed architectural trust boundaries within Trusted Execution Environments (TEEs) and confidential computing. He argued that claims of cloud providers being completely outside the Trusted Computing Base (TCB) are invalid due to dependencies on hypervisor identity provisioning, VM firmware, side-channel vulnerabilities, and relay attacks. The presentation highlighted implications for digital sovereignty and invited alignment with the RATS working group.

* **[Why Should a Young Engineer Join the IETF?](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-hotrfc-sessa-why-should-a-young-engineer-join-the-ietf-00)**
  * **Presenter**: Jean Francois Guerrero
  * **Discussion**: Jean Francois Guerrero presented demographic data indicating the median age of IETF participants is between 45 and 54, alongside low retention rates for first-time attendees. He introduced initiatives by the IA Foundation, including university technology clubs, remote participation hubs, and specialized fellowships in Southeast Asia. He urged companies, academics, and SDOs to create structured career pathways and dedicated internships for young engineers in standardization.

